This is partner content.

The Houston native sits down with Billboard and Intuit TurboTax to discuss his early beginnings as a DJ, his evolving relationship with earning, and how he manages yearly income and revenue — especially during tax season — while continuing to grow and diversify his catalog.

Chase B: I&#8217;m Chase B and this is Then and Now presented by Intuit TurboTax. DJing really specifically entered my world, definitely in college when I was at Howard. I started at the radio station WFDC, and I started as an MC. I just picked up on so many things being next to the DJ and I would practice for like 10-11 hours a day. And that&#8217;s when I really just kind of fell in love with the whole the whole culture of the sneakers, the fashion, the music, you know, just all was so important to me. When I was in New York, my first like, kind of, like, consistent. I won&#8217;t even call it a check, because you might as well do it for free at that point. I&#8217;d be like to open a DJ for these huge acts, like, you know, like, Chief Keefs of the world, all that type of stuff thing. I remember, like, all right, I&#8217;m starting get at least a little bit of income. And, you know, it was rough for a while. And then, when I started getting, like, those first, like, club gigs, is when things started pick up. I had a friend that was looking and he come one day, like, &#8220;The DJ had to cancel, like, can you come and basically, like, pick up his shift?&#8221; So I basically picked up his shift, so I did that and I was like, &#8220;Oh, I just made $300 to do this. And I think I&#8217;m on to something.&#8221; You know, and then he come back the next day and was like, &#8220;The DJ canceled again.&#8221; That&#8217;s when things were getting consistent. And I was like, &#8220;Oh, this is my actual career now.&#8221;
